# Substrate compose env — copy to .env and fill in. The .env file is gitignored.
#
# Organised by TIER, not alphabetically. Read only as far as your topology needs:
#   TIER 1  you cannot start without deciding this          (one variable)
#   TIER 2  needed for a specific topology                  (spoke / second instance / providers)
#   TIER 3  tuning most operators never touch
#
# To see what the substrate ACTUALLY resolved — and whether your value won or a
# previously-persisted one beat it:
#   docker exec substrate-live substrate-config
# ("persisted" in the source column means a PRIOR boot's value is still in force.)

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# TIER 1 — REQUIRED. This is the whole tier.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# One LLM provider key. A SPOKE needs none — it resolves models on its hub.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...

# OpenAI-compatible alternative (Ollama, vLLM, LM Studio, …) instead of the above:
# OPENAI_API_KEY=ollama
# OPENAI_BASE_URL=http://localhost:11434/v1
# LLM_DEFAULT_MODEL=llama3.2

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# TIER 2a — JOIN AN EXISTING HUB (spoke)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Point-and-go: these three are sufficient. Identity, trace store and the relay
# anchor are all fetched from <discovery-endpoint>/bootstrap at boot.
#
# METABOB_API_KEY is issued BY THE HUB — mint it there with:
#   docker exec <hub-container> substrate-key issue <this-spoke>
# Omitting it is now a boot error rather than a silent local key that the hub
# would reject on every call.
# METABOB_API_KEY=
# DISCOVERY_ENDPOINT=http://<hub-host>:18100
# HUB_DISCOVERY_URL=http://<hub-host>:18100
# ENABLED_ROLES=spoke
#
# Optional (auto-resolved from /bootstrap; set only to override):
# ACTIVITY_API_ENDPOINT=http://<hub-host>:18080
# IDENTITY_VESSEL_URL=http://<hub-host>:18101
# FED_SUBSTRATE_ID=
# RELAY_MULTIADDR=          # a hand-pinned multiaddr goes STALE on a relay restart

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# TIER 2b — A SECOND SUBSTRATE ON THIS HOST
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# For the compose path, set a distinct container name, every host port, AND the
# two volume names.
#
# ⚠ THE VOLUME NAMES ARE NOT OPTIONAL. docker-compose.yml names its volumes
# explicitly so it adopts an existing fleet instead of stranding it — which
# means a second fleet that sets only the container name and ports mounts the
# DEFAULT substrate-workspace / substrate-surreal, i.e. the FIRST fleet's
# learning state. Two substrates writing one datastore corrupts both.
#
#   WORKSPACE_VOLUME=<name>-workspace
#   SURREAL_VOLUME=<name>-surreal
# (The `make up` path has a shortcut: LIVE_NAME=<name> PORT_OFFSET=<n>, where n
#  is added to the 18xxx HOST port — not the internal one. Keep 18310+n well
#  under 32768: above that the fleet lands inside the ephemeral port range and
#  you get intermittent `bind: address already in use` on ports nothing is
#  listening on. 5000-12000 is a good range.)
#
# The offset is +10000 from the internal port: 8080 -> 18080. Shift all NINE or
# the two fleets collide.
# SUBSTRATE_CONTAINER=substrate-scratch
# ACTIVITY_API_PORT=18080
# DEV_VESSEL_PORT=18090
# DISCOVERY_PORT=18100
# IDENTITY_PORT=18101
# GOAL_HOST_PORT=18210
# ANALYSIS_PORT=18250
# CONCEPT_DB_PORT=18260
# STATEFUL_UI_PORT=18270
# HUMAN_SURFACE_PORT=18310   # the human surface — the one a person talks to

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# TIER 2c — EXTRA LLM PROVIDERS
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# gen-env advertises one resolver arm per key present. An arm whose key is
# absent is skipped cleanly, so a keyless host simply grows fewer arms.
# OPENAI_API_KEY=
# GOOGLE_API_KEY=
# GROQ_API_KEY=
# MISTRAL_API_KEY=
# OPENROUTER_API_KEY=
# CHUTES_API_KEY=
#
# Replace the ENTIRE arm list without editing a tracked file (JSON array of
# {id, model, provider, port}); this wins over scripts/substrate/llm-arms.json:
# LLM_ARMS=

# Self-hosted vLLM. Single instance: VLLM_BASE_URL + VLLM_MODELS (comma-separated
# served ids), VLLM_API_KEY only if the server runs with --api-key. Multiple:
# VLLM_ENDPOINTS as [{"id":..,"baseURL":..,"models":[..]}].
# VLLM_BASE_URL=
# VLLM_MODELS=
# VLLM_API_KEY=
# VLLM_ENDPOINTS=

# RunPod Serverless (scale-to-zero vLLM). The arm is offered to the policy ONLY
# while a worker is already warm — a cold hit pays a 20-40 min image-pull and
# engine-load tail, which is a capacity fact, not a price, so it is gated rather
# than charged for. RUNPOD_COST_PER_MTOK is a PRIOR, not a measurement: RunPod
# bills GPU-seconds and publishes no per-token rate.
# RUNPOD_ENDPOINT_ID=
# RUNPOD_API_KEY=
# RUNPOD_MODELS=
# RUNPOD_COST_PER_MTOK=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# TIER 2d — VESSEL SELECTION (which units run at all)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Precedence, highest first:
#   PROFILE > ENABLED_VESSELS > ENABLED_ROLES,  then + EXTRA,  then - DISABLED
#
# An unknown PROFILE or role name is now a BOOT ERROR listing the valid names,
# rather than a container that silently comes up nearly empty.
#
# Role groups: hub | spoke | full. Bare roles include: store, control, api,
# compute, models, ui, transport, seed, infra, autonomy, registry.
# NOTE `models` names the LLM resolver arms. `spoke` deliberately EXCLUDES it —
# a spoke resolves models on its hub — while hub and full include it.
# PROFILE=
# ENABLED_VESSELS=
# ENABLED_ROLES=
# ENABLED_EXTRA_VESSELS=
# DISABLED_VESSELS=
#
# Preview a selection without booting:
#   docker exec <container> env DRY_RUN=1 ENABLED_ROLES=spoke apply-inventory

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# TIER 2e — SAFETY SWITCHES
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# UNSET MEANS ON. The substrate authors code changes, commits them, and pushes
# to origin/dev of the vessel clone. Set to 0 to hold it to intent-only.
# MITOSIS_DIRECT_PUSH=
# Route edit-intent goals to feature_compose (the autonomous edit-landing path).
# ROUTE_EDIT_INTENT_TO_COMPOSE=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# TIER 2f — SELF-DEVELOPMENT (pull + push on the source repos)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Fine-grained PAT. Contents:Read = self-update (pull); Contents:Read+Write =
# self-push (land its own commits). Without it the substrate still runs, from a
# read-only baked snapshot, and self-authored commits stay local.
SUBSTRATE_GIT_PAT=
GITHUB_TOKEN=
# SUBSTRATE_REPO_OWNER=AviGopal

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# TIER 2g — PUBLIC HUB
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# PUBLIC_IP is what makes discovery's /bootstrap advertise a reachable address
# instead of loopback, and the federation relay HARD-EXITS without it — under
# Restart=always, so the symptom is a permanent crash-loop that reports
# `activating` and never `failed`. No launch path passes it; set it here, or on
# the container's /etc/substrate/env, before installing federation-relay.
#
# (An earlier version of this comment referred to "*_PUBLIC_* overrides below".
# There are none in this file, and none are forwarded by compose or make.)
# PUBLIC_IP=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# TIER 3 — tuning
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# IMAGE. The ghcr package is PUBLIC — no docker login, no PAT needed to pull.
# :dev is the fleet; :obsidian additionally carries an in-container desktop.
# SUBSTRATE_IMAGE=ghcr.io/avigopal/substrate:dev
#
# The HMAC secret identity-vessel signs and verifies every mb- key with.
# Generate with `openssl rand -hex 32`. On a FRESH datastore this is generated
# and persisted for you; on an EXISTING one with no persisted value, gen-env
# refuses to boot rather than fall back to the public legacy default. Writing it
# into /workspace/.substrate-secrets works too, and is what the error suggests.
# API_KEY_SECRET=
# API_KEY_SECRET_PREVIOUS=      # only during a deliberate rotation
#
# Escape hatch for an existing deployment still on the legacy default signing
# secret. Keys remain FORGEABLE while this is set — prefer API_KEY_SECRET above.
# ALLOW_INSECURE_API_KEY_SECRET=1

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# STATE
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Two named volumes hold everything durable: substrate-workspace (/workspace)
# and substrate-surreal (/var/lib/surrealdb — traces, posteriors, concept graph).
# They survive `docker rm`; deleting them destroys all learning state.
#
# docker-compose.yml names both volumes explicitly, so compose attaches to the
# same substrate-workspace / substrate-surreal every other launch path uses and
# ADOPTS an existing fleet rather than replacing it. Give a second fleet its own
# state by setting WORKSPACE_VOLUME and SURREAL_VOLUME alongside
# SUBSTRATE_CONTAINER. Check what a container really holds:
#   docker inspect <container> --format '{{range .Mounts}}{{.Name}} {{end}}'
#
# Read the operator API key after launch:
#   docker exec substrate-live substrate-key show
